6. COMPLETE COVERAGE OF ALL SIX SYLLABUS TOPICS
Use these academic boundaries when inventing the puzzles.
Location 1: Memory System and Basic Concepts
Explore the foundations of computer memory.
Include relevant concepts such as memory's purpose, the role of memory in computer systems, storage capacity and units, memory access, read/write operations, latency, bandwidth, volatile versus non-volatile memory, and other relevant basic concepts.
Choose which concepts are most appropriate for the five puzzles.
Make the player interact with the memory system rather than simply memorizing definitions.
Location 2: Memory Hierarchy
Explore how different storage levels are organized according to properties such as speed, capacity, cost per bit, and proximity to the processor.
Include relevant levels such as registers, cache, main memory, and secondary storage, together with their relationships.
Invent environmental puzzles that require the player to understand why a hierarchy exists and how its levels cooperate.
Be academically precise. Avoid oversimplifying the hierarchy in ways that introduce incorrect claims.
Location 3: Main Memory
Explore the structure, purpose, operation, and relevant types of main memory.
Include applicable concepts such as RAM, SRAM, DRAM, memory addressing, read/write operations, addressable locations, and memory capacity calculations.
Select relevant concepts and invent puzzles that require their application.
Keep the scope appropriate to the stated syllabus. Do not introduce unrelated advanced topics merely to make the game appear more technical.
Location 4: Secondary Storage
Explore persistent data storage and the characteristics of secondary-storage devices.
Include relevant concepts such as non-volatility, storage capacity, access characteristics, and the differences between suitable storage technologies, including HDDs and SSDs where relevant.
Create puzzles around the properties and behavior of storage systems, not simply device-name memorization.
Location 5: Cache Memory Mapping
Explore how main-memory blocks map into cache.
Include the applicable mapping techniques: direct mapping, fully associative mapping, and set-associative mapping.
Where relevant, incorporate cache lines, blocks, tags, index fields, offset fields, address decomposition, and mapping calculations.
Invent puzzles involving physical or logical cache structures, address-routing challenges, and other suitable mechanics.
All calculations must be correct. Define any assumptions needed to solve a puzzle, including address size, block size, cache-line count, and mapping rules.
Do not confuse memory blocks with cache lines or incorrectly apply mapping formulas.
Location 6: Cache Coherence
Explore the problem of maintaining consistent data when multiple processors or caches hold copies of shared information.
Include relevant concepts such as stale cached data, updates, shared data, and appropriate coherence mechanisms or protocols within the syllabus's intended depth.
Create interactive scenarios in which the player must reason about the state of cached copies and how consistency can be restored.
Avoid teaching incorrect rules about when a cache line becomes invalid, modified, shared, or synchronized.
Introduce protocol-specific state names or transition rules only when the relevant protocol has been clearly explained within the game.
Coverage verification
Before considering the game complete, create an internal checklist mapping every major syllabus topic to the actual puzzles that teach or test it.
Verify that all six locations contain five substantive puzzles each, that the puzzle content is accurate, and that no syllabus topic is accidentally omitted.
The final experience must cover the complete supplied Module 5 syllabus, not merely its most visually interesting topics.
